EXT. ELIAB'S FARM - GATE - DUSK #13#

Eliab gives the evening orders across the yard. Hired men coming in, oxen, smoke from the ovens.

He goes to the niche and strikes a light for the lamp. As the flame catches, he looks down the road.

Far off, a figure, barefoot and limping, and still walking the way only one person walks.

Eliab drops the flint. He runs.

JESUS (V.O.)
(over the run)
But when he was yet a great way off, his father saw him, and had compassion, and ran, and fell on his neck, and kissed him.

An old man, his mantle hitched up in his fist, runs out of his own gate and down the long road in front of his servants, the dog bounding at his heels. The servants stare, then follow. Below, at the village gate, the old men on the bench get to their feet. The whole village watches him run.

EXT. THE ROAD - DUSK

Asher looks up the road. Against the low sun a man is coming toward him, running: black, faceless, a shape and nothing more.

Asher stops dead.

The shape comes on, and as it reaches him the face comes out of the light: old, wet with tears, his father's.

Eliab falls on his neck, and kisses him. The grip finds the cracked ribs and Asher gasps; his father does not let go. Asher stands stiff in his arms, and then he doesn't.

Eliab takes his son's face in his hands and sees the split brow, the blood dried black in it.

ELIAB
Asher.

ASHER
Father, I have sinned against heaven, and in your sight, and I am no longer worthy to be called your son.

Before he can say the rest, Eliab turns to the servants running down the road behind him: Micah, Dan, Shallum, Miriam.

ELIAB
Bring out the best robe, and put it on him;

Micah turns back at a run. Eliab twists the signet off his own finger and presses it into Micah's palm as he goes.

ELIAB
and put a ring on his hand, and shoes on his feet.

Dan runs.

ELIAB
And bring the fatted calf, and kill it; and let us eat, and make merry.

Shallum runs.

EXT. ELIAB'S FARM - COURTYARD - NIGHT

Torches. The household gathering, neighbours coming up the hill with lamps. Micah pulls the best robe over Asher's head; it is too long in the sleeve. He works the ring over the swollen knuckles of the hand that took the bread.

Miriam kneels with a basin of warm water and takes his cut, filthy feet in her hands. The water goes dark.

MIRIAM
(through tears)
You are late, Asher.

Dan kneels with the sandals.

Eliab turns to the whole yard.

ELIAB
For this my son was dead, and lives again; he was lost, and is found.

The drum starts. Then the pipe. Then everyone.

